+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Competing Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited by more than one worker from the same starting state before either submission reaches the server — tablets hold drafts offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store must decide what happens when two submissions for the same visit arrive with the same starting point: whether to keep both submitted visits intact for a coordinator to choose between, or to try to combine them automatically.
+
+A visit's scientific fields — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes — are not independent of each other in ways a field-level rule can see. A revised species identification and a revised cover estimate for that species are two different fields but one scientific correction; a rule that combines them because they don't touch the same field name can produce a record that matches neither worker's intent and that no one reviewed. This decision determines whether that risk is possible at all, and it sizes real work in two other nodes: tablet-sync's local storage and retry handling, and review-export's coordinator interface and export eligibility rules. Both are designed after this node and inherit whichever shape this decision produces.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No submitted edit is ever discarded.** (Inherited — project tenet.) An option fails this gate if any accepted submission can be overwritten or dropped without being retained somewhere a coordinator can still see it.
+- **Every exported value traces to a specific submitted revision.** (Inherited — project tenet: *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.) An option fails this gate if a value that reaches export can exist without a single worker's submission that produced it verbatim.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Risk of an unreviewed, scientifically incoherent combination** (major — the concern that settled this decision). Does the mechanism ever produce a record from two submissions' fields without a human having looked at that specific combination?
+- **Review workload** (major — ties to the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). How much of the ~6,000 visits/season volume lands in a coordinator's queue versus becoming export-ready on its own?
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate). Does the mechanism need rules that have to be kept correct as the schema evolves (species entries, observer lists), or none?
+- **Export readiness by Friday** (moderate). How many visits are blocked from the weekly CSV by edits that two coordinators haven't had time to look at?
+- **Submission size over an offline connection** (minor). Tablets reconnect after up to 48 hours offline; does the mechanism require sending a full payload or a structured diff?
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it was edited from. A submission whose named revision still matches the visit's current revision becomes current cleanly. A submission whose named revision does not match — because another submission already became current first — is kept as a competing revision alongside the current one, and the visit waits for a coordinator to pick which revision stands. No submission's fields are ever combined with another's; a visit's current state is always exactly one worker's submitted revision.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No-discard gate: **passes** — every revision, current or competing, is retained indefinitely.
+- Inspectable-export gate: **passes** — the current revision at export time is always one worker's verbatim submission.
+- Risk of unreviewed combination: **strong fit** — the mechanism has no combination step, so this risk cannot occur.
+- Review workload: **weak fit** — any two submissions that overlap in time on the same visit reach the queue, even when they touch unrelated fields (an observer-name fix and a missing-species addition still compete).
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **strong fit** — no field-type rules to write or keep correct as the schema changes.
+- Export readiness: **weak fit** — a visit with any competing revision is held out of export until resolved, regardless of whether the competing edits were substantively related.
+- Submission size: **partial fit** — each submission sends the full visit payload rather than a diff, though a single visit record is small enough that this is not a binding constraint at this volume.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and the fields it changed. Disjoint field changes (e.g., an observer-name fix and a missing-species addition) combine automatically into a single new current revision. Changes that touch the same field, or fields the merge rules treat as linked, go to review instead.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No-discard gate: **passes**, if both original submissions are retained alongside the auto-combined result — the option does not require discarding either.
+- Inspectable-export gate: **conditional.** If an auto-combined record is allowed to reach export without a coordinator having looked at that specific combination, the gate is **failed** — the exported value traces to a combination no human reviewed, not to a single submitted revision. The gate can be made to pass by requiring manual approval of every auto-combined record before export — but that removes most of this option's review-workload advantage, since the coordinator ends up looking at every combination anyway.
+- Risk of unreviewed combination: **weak fit** — the entire value of the option is combining fields the server did not flag, and the server cannot distinguish an unrelated pair of fields (observer name, missing species) from a related pair (a species identification and its own cover estimate) without rules that encode scientific relationships the team has not written down and would have to maintain. A rule set that doesn't know a given pair of fields is linked will combine them silently.
+- Review workload: **strong fit**, *if* auto-combination is allowed to stand without manual approval — disjoint edits never reach a coordinator.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **weak fit** — needs maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and those rules have to be revisited every time the visit schema or the team's sense of which fields are scientifically linked changes.
+- Export readiness: **strong fit** — more visits are export-ready without coordinator action.
+- Submission size: **strong fit** — a structured diff is smaller than a full payload, though as with the other option this isn't binding at current volume.
+
+**Summary**: trades a smaller review queue and faster export readiness for a maintained rule set that can combine two submissions' fields into a record no one reviewed.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The deciding factor is the risk-of-unreviewed-combination criterion, which the field-aware option cannot win without surrendering its own main advantage: either it auto-combines without review — and the inspectable-export gate fails whenever the combined fields were scientifically linked in a way the rules didn't know to check — or it routes every auto-combination through manual approval, at which point it no longer reduces review workload relative to whole-visit revisions and only adds maintained rules for no offsetting benefit. A mechanism that cannot win on its own strongest criterion without conceding its other strong criterion is not a live choice between two comparably good options; whole-visit revisions is preferred even though it loses outright on review workload and export readiness — a bigger review queue is an operational cost the team can see and staff for, while a silently miscombined scientific record is a correctness failure the team would not see until a regional science partner questioned it.
+
+This decision turns on *do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* — both read narrowly enough here that "discard" and "inspectable" extend to never producing a value that didn't come from one worker's own submission, not only to never deleting data. It is taken in tension with *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*, accepting that the review queue will be larger than it would be under merging.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID a draft was edited from, resubmit it unchanged on retry, and be able to show a worker that their submission is pending alongside a competing one rather than silently superseded or merged.
+- **Review-export** must support a revision-comparison view (current revision against every competing revision, each with its author and receipt time) and must hold any visit with unresolved competing revisions out of the Friday CSV entirely — there is no partial or auto-approved export path for a visit mid-review.
+
+Revisit this decision if review volume or coordinator capacity changes enough that the review-workload cost becomes the binding constraint — at that point, re-examine whether a narrower, explicitly-scoped auto-combine rule (e.g., only for a short allowlist of field pairs the team has explicitly confirmed are independent) could pass the inspectable-export gate without reintroducing the silent-combination risk this decision rejected.
